  "strategy": "## Approach and Rationale\n\nThe fundamental challenge is minimizing the maximum overlap integral \\(C_5\\) for a step function \\(h(x)\\) over \\(x \\in [0, 2]\\) subject to \\(h(x) \\in [0, 1]\\) and \\(\\int_0^2 h(x) \\, dx = 1\\).\n\n### Central Idea\n\nI will employ a **multi-resolution multi-start optimization strategy** combined with **coordinate-wise updates** to distort the parent solution's oscillatory pattern effectively. The key insight is that minimal overlap occurs with a function that either (a) is concentrated in a small region, reducing the number of possible overlapping shifts, or (b) distributes support to minimize the height products \\(h(x)h(x+k)\\). Though concentrated distributions initially seem promising, adjacent peaks inevitably cause overlap, so the optimal lies in carefully weighting peak separation versus height.\n\n### Non-Convex Optimization Challenges\n\nThe objective involves:\n\n1. A max operation over all possible shifts \\(k\\)\n2. A nonlinear integral constraint\n3. Product terms \\(h(x)h(x+k)\\)\n\nTogether, these create a non-convex optimization landscape. I cannot rely on simple gradient-based methods. Instead, I use:\n\n- **Resolution hopping**: Test multiple grid sizes (40, 60, 80, 100)\n- **Starting diversity**: Initialize from parent solution and random checks\n- **Adaptive constraints**: Use probability-based weighting of the integral constraint\n- **Orthonormal basis projection**: Projects/repair immediately maintains integrals\n- **Entropy-based exploration**: To segment local ridges\n\nThe algorithm anticipates long execution times, so resource-efficient interpolation and smart grid selection are critical.\n\n### Time/Budget Considerations\n\nOptimization scale with \\(O(n_{\\text{points}}^2)\\) correlation. I plan:\n\n- Early tests with small grids (\\(n_{\\text{points}}\\)=40, d\u00e0y 60) to identify promising patterns\n- Then iterate with escalating grid sizes\n- Up to \\(n_{\\text{points}}\\)=100 as the failure point given 1300 seconds\n\nThis balances exploration and computation.\n\n### Fallback Strategy\n\nIf time runs short, the algorithm falls back to projecting the parent solution to available grid sizes, then refining via short optimization runs derived from numerical differentiation.",
